    The Characterization of Hermes 

\vspace{10pt}

    The twelve great Olympians are commonly thought to be beautiful, powerful, and 

\vspace{10pt}

    perfect. However, this isn’t always the case. Hermes, the Greek god of merchants, thieves, and 

\vspace{10pt}

    travelers, is far from perfect. Like everyday people, he faces challenges, makes mistakes, and 

\vspace{10pt}

    learns things about the world as he embarks on various adventures. Based on D’Aulaires’ Book 

\vspace{10pt}

    of Greek Myths and Stephanie Spinner’s novel Quicksilver, Hermes is characterized as clever, 

\vspace{10pt}

    humorous, and caring. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Firstly, Hermes is characterized as clever. According to the novel, Hermes began playing 

\vspace{10pt}

    tricks since he was a newborn on Mount Cyllene. The following describes the tricks newborn 

\vspace{10pt}

    Hermes played on his brother Apollo. One example of this is when Hermes sets out to steal 

\vspace{10pt}

    Apollo’s cows. The book of Greek myths states, “To keep Apollo from knowing who had stolen 

\vspace{10pt}

    his herd and which way they had been driven, Hermes slyly wrapped the hoofs of the cows with 

\vspace{10pt}

    bark to disguise their prints, and tied brooms to their tails so they would erase their own tracks. 

\vspace{10pt}

    To confuse Apollo even further, he drove the cows backward out of the pasture, and tied bundles 

\vspace{10pt}

    of branches to his own little feet so it looked as if a giant had led something into the pasture, but 

\vspace{10pt}

    nothing out” (D’Aulaires, 49). This shows that Hermes was clever, even as a little child. With 

\vspace{10pt}

    only being a few hours old, he was able to trick his much more mature brother, Apollo. All of his 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    actions are outstanding compared to an average newborn his age. Most impressively, Hermes had 

\vspace{10pt}

    a well-thought out plan and backup plan. Additionally, Hermes showed that he is clever later in 

\vspace{10pt}

    life. Hades, god of the underworld, had stolen Kore, Demeter’s daughter, and was forcing her to 

\vspace{10pt}

    become his wife. When the gods found out, Demeter, the goddess of harvest, gets angry and 

\vspace{10pt}

    threatens to stop all growth on Earth until Kore returns to her. To solve the problem, Hermes 

\vspace{10pt}

    responds, "Say that Kore has to stay with Hades half the year because she ate the pomegranate 

\vspace{10pt}

    seeds but that she can spend the rest of the year with Demeter" (Spinner, 45). This is an example 

\vspace{10pt}

    of Hermes using his cleverness positively in a tough circumstance. As the messenger god, 

\vspace{10pt}

    Hermes is truly persuasive, diplomatic, and sharp when it comes to making deals and 

\vspace{10pt}

    agreements. Hermes was the only one capable of coming up with a compromise that worked for 

\vspace{10pt}

    both sides. These are just some examples where Hermes uses his cleverness to his advantage in 

\vspace{10pt}

