    Hermes is also a humorous character. Apollo, the god of sun and light, was enraged that 

\vspace{10pt}

    his baby brother stole his best cows. According to the Greek myth, “All the gods burst out 

\vspace{10pt}

    laughing when they saw innocent-looking little Hermes running with furious Apollo at his heels” 

\vspace{10pt}

    (D’ Aulaires, 53). Right as he was born, Hermes established his silly reputation. Hermes was 

\vspace{10pt}

    capable of humoring all the gods, including Zeus, who is usually quite stolid. From then on, 

\vspace{10pt}

    Hermes tries to use his humor as a way to gain his father’s attention and appreciation. Years later, 

\vspace{10pt}

    Hermes arrives in Hell for his mission to steal Kore back from Hades. This is because Hades had 

\vspace{10pt}

    kidnapped Demeter’s daughter, Kore, to be his wife. Hermes described his popularity among the 

\vspace{10pt}

    dead souls. “They’re overjoyed to see me when I visit because I tell them jokes. And I have to 

\vspace{10pt}

    admit they’re my best audience. They like puns. They like riddles. They even like mortal jokes” 

\vspace{10pt}

    (Spinner, 18). Hell is known to be one of the darkest, most boring places in the world. By 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    bringing laughter to the dead, Hermes shows that his funny personality can light up even the 

\vspace{10pt}

    darkest places. This allows many to take a liking for him, and this helps him create strong 

\vspace{10pt}

    relationships with others. In conclusion, Hermes uses his humorous personality to make many 

\vspace{10pt}

