    Finally, caring is another one of Hermes’ traits. As mentioned before, Hermes stole 50 of 

\vspace{10pt}

    Apollo’s best cows as a newborn. After playing his tricks on Apollo, Hermes reflects on his 

\vspace{10pt}

    actions to his wary mother. Hermes said to his mother Maia, “I did what I had to do for you and 

\vspace{10pt}

    me. We don’t want to live in this dark cave forever. Soon I will be seated on high Olympus as 

\vspace{10pt}

    one of the twelve great gods, and you too will live there in glory as my mother” (D’ Aulaires, 51). 

\vspace{10pt}

    This shows that Hermes not only wants good and honor for himself, but for his mother as well. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Maia had to endure lots of hardships in order to give birth to him, as she would have been 

\vspace{10pt}

    targeted by Zeus’s wife, Hera. They live in a dark cave on Mount Cyllene where no one knows 

\vspace{10pt}

    about Hermes. Hermes is caring by being loyal to his mother, and being thankful for all that she 

\vspace{10pt}

    has done for him. Another example of this is after Hermes successfully aids Perseus, Zeus’s 

\vspace{10pt}

    mortal child, with slaying the gorgon Medusa. Hermes retells, “As I stared at her lifeless body, 

\vspace{10pt}

    my revulsion became sorrow—heavy, dark, guilt-laden. I hadn’t killed Medusa, but I had made 

\vspace{10pt}

    her death possible. This is hateful, | thought. I'll never do it again. With the vow my spirits 

\vspace{10pt}

    lifted” (Spinner, 74). This is interesting, since Hermes should be proud that he had accomplished 

\vspace{10pt}

    his task— to help Perseus slay Medusa. However, he still feels empathy for Medusa, as he still 

\vspace{10pt}

    sees her as the young girl who had been cursed into a monster. By showing compassion for 

\vspace{10pt}

    someone he is sworn to feel disgusted by, Hermes is showing that he cares for all living beings. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Ultimately, these two examples prove that Hermes cares deeply for those closest to him, and he 

\vspace{10pt}

    also cares for those who he doesn’t know well. 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    In both D ’Aulaires’ Book of Greek Myths and Quicksilver by Stephanie Spinner, Hermes 

\vspace{10pt}

    is portrayed as a clever, humorous, and caring character. These traits are evident as Hermes 

\vspace{10pt}

    embarks on many journeys, develops relationships, and makes discoveries. Interestingly, all of 

\vspace{10pt}

    these are aspects of human life. Just like everyday people, Hermes has a personality, overcomes 

\vspace{10pt}

    challenges, and is not perfect. 

\vspace{10pt}

